!!id:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/40/orders/orders-api-inline-1.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/io/ltr8/http/service.tn"
!!import:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/40/orders/orders-types-1.tn"
!!import:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/40/orders/orders-errors-1.tn"
@doc:"""
  Web service only: an http projection on its own terms, with no protocol behind it and no `binds`, so every
  endpoint is a `!service.operation` carrying its signature inline. The verb and the path are the keys an endpoint
  sits under; a path key is a name in the URL's own grammar, so `/orders/{id}` needs no identifier minted
  from it. A parameter-only endpoint is a one-field request record placed from the path.

  An exchange's facts ride on its own key, so on an inline operation they sit before the verb: a GET must be
  `@service.safe`, and the reader refuses one that is not.

  Serving a schema at its identity's path answers bytes rather than a TSON value, so it is a `!service.document`
  endpoint and not an operation; the demos' own description (demo/schemas/orders-api-1.tn) declares one.

  A resource is written either way: positionally, its map of verbs standing in for its one field, or tagged
  `!service.resource`, as the last one is.
  """
!schema {
  orders_http => !service.http {
    resources: {
      "/orders" => {
        @doc:"Place an order. The whole body is the order."
        POST => !service.operation { request: order  response: order  errors: [sku_not_found]  status: 201 }
      }
      "/orders/{id}" => {
        @service.safe
        @doc:"One order, by the id in the path."
        GET => !service.operation { request: order_ref  response: order  errors: [order_not_found] }
      }
      "/orders/{id}/items" => !service.resource {
        @service.safe
        @doc:"""
          The lines of one order: a second parameter-only endpoint, and a nested path written as a template of
          its own -- the URL namespace is hierarchical and a resource key is flat over it, which is the
          qualified-name question wearing a slash.
          """
        GET => !service.operation { request: order_ref  response: order_page }
      }
    }
  }
}
